### Preserve the tracked policy when cleanup skips a release (1/3)

**Severity:** `medium`

Please keep the held-key policy when cleanup does not inject a release. Keyboard Manager can suppress an incoming modifier while injecting its remapped non-modifier, so the original modifier can be reported up even though its remote press is still active. Removing this entry lets a later repeat/key-up use a changed bypass setting; the matching key-up can then skip the remapper that handled key-down, leaving the target held. Add a release decision here and leave the entry intact when it is false. Apply all three coordinated suggestions together.

```suggestion
        internal static bool ConsumeRemoteKeyboardBypassPolicy(int vkCode, bool releaseKey = true)
        {
            lock (RemoteKeyboardBypassLock)
            {
                return releaseKey && RemoteKeyboardBypassPolicies.Remove(vkCode, out bool bypassKeyboardManager)
```

### Use the same cleanup decision for consumption and injection (2/3)

**Severity:** `medium`

Determine whether this cleanup path will inject a release before consuming the policy, and pass that same decision to the helper in (1/3). A source modifier reported up must retain its policy for the eventual remote key-up; a modifier actually released by cleanup should still consume its saved policy. Apply with (1/3) and (3/3).

